# Interception Document — API.md §Interception, ADR-0008
#
# `clients` and `exclude_domains` do NOT live in fastadhunter.toml. They live
# in /config/interception.json and are read and replaced through this pair.
# A PUT applies on the next accepted connection: no restart, and the response
# carries no `restart_required` field.
#
# PUT replaces the WHOLE document. A missing key is an empty list, so
# `{"clients": ["..."]}` clears every exclusion.
#
# Caps: 256 clients, 512 exclude_domains. A rejected PUT changes nothing —
# neither the file nor the running scope.
#
# Errors carry a structured `details` object whose `reason` is one of
# `shape`, `over_cap`, `invalid_entry`, `duplicate`:
#
#   {"error": {"code": "validation_failed",
#              "message": "clients[3]: \"10.0.0.300\" is not an IP address or CIDR block",
#              "details": {"reason": "invalid_entry", "list": "clients",
#                          "index": 3, "entry": "10.0.0.300"}}}

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### What is in force right now. The document the API shows and the scope the
### HTTPS listener reads are the same value — they cannot disagree.
GET {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}

### List two managed clients and splice one bank. The stored spelling is what
### you sent; only the compiled matcher is normalised.
PUT {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "clients": ["192.168.88.10", "192.168.88.0/24"],
  "exclude_domains": ["unicredit.ro", "revolut.com"]
}

### Intercept nobody, keep the exclusions. Machinery stays built, so adding
### the first client back is one PUT away — still no restart.
PUT {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "clients": [],
  "exclude_domains": ["unicredit.ro", "revolut.com"]
}

### 422 invalid_entry: the index and the entry are the operator's own.
PUT {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "clients": ["192.168.88.10", "10.0.0.300"]
}

### 422 duplicate: "Bank.ro" and "bank.ro." are the same host after
### normalization; `duplicate_of` points at the first spelling.
PUT {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "exclude_domains": ["Bank.ro", "bank.ro."]
}

### 422 shape: unknown keys are refused rather than silently dropped.
PUT {{base}}/interception
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client": ["192.168.88.10"]
}

### 422 from POST /config: the two keys have exactly one owner, and it is the
### endpoint above.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "https": { "interception": { "clients": ["192.168.88.10"] } }
}
